WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.472
Code review for XSS prevention should focus on rendering decisions.

2
00:00:04.472 --> 00:00:17.088
Raw output, manually assembled markup, broad display helpers, custom encoders, rich text rendering, markdown rendering, CMS display, and client-side DOM shortcuts deserve special attention.

3
00:00:17.088 --> 00:00:27.567
Reviewers do not need offensive payload examples to ask the right question: what context is this value rendered into, and which approved pattern protects it?

4
00:00:27.567 --> 00:00:30.704
Shared components multiply risk and protection.

5
00:00:30.704 --> 00:00:41.651
Layouts, partial views, view components, tag helpers, reusable table components, notification components, form helpers, and display templates may affect many pages.

6
00:00:41.651 --> 00:00:44.989
A safe shared helper can reduce repeated mistakes.

7
00:00:44.989 --> 00:00:49.928
An unsafe shared helper can spread risky rendering across the application.

8
00:00:49.928 --> 00:00:52.932
Dependencies are part of the rendering model.

9
00:00:52.932 --> 00:01:06.148
UI packages, rich text editors, markdown renderers, sanitizers, template engines, client-side frameworks, and server-side helpers should be reviewed for maintenance, configuration, and approved use.

10
00:01:06.148 --> 00:01:16.427
A dependency that handles content should have an owner, an update path, and a clear understanding of which safety guarantees it does and does not provide.

11
00:01:16.427 --> 00:01:20.099
Legacy code often contains older rendering assumptions.

12
00:01:20.099 --> 00:01:32.981
It may have hand-built markup, helper methods that predate current framework conventions, broad trusted-content flags, or views that combine server data with client code in hard-to-review ways.

13
00:01:32.981 --> 00:01:42.526
Migration should move those areas toward Razor defaults, context-aware helpers, operation-specific view models, and documented rich text paths.

14
00:01:42.526 --> 00:01:47.265
Testing can support prevention without becoming payload-heavy training.

15
00:01:47.265 --> 00:01:56.143
Tests can verify that ordinary display names, comments, descriptions, and support messages are rendered as text in expected contexts.

16
00:01:56.143 --> 00:02:09.359
They can also cover rich text policies, component behavior, and regression cases from previous defects using safe fixtures and assertions about rendered structure rather than exploit demonstrations.

17
00:02:09.359 --> 00:02:12.029
Logging and diagnostics need review too.

18
00:02:12.029 --> 00:02:22.175
Applications sometimes preserve raw user content in logs, error pages, debug panels, or support exports and later render that content in internal tools.

19
00:02:22.175 --> 00:02:29.784
Logs should provide useful context without becoming a place where untrusted content is reinterpreted by a browser.

20
00:02:29.784 --> 00:02:34.790
Defensive review includes both customer-facing pages and operational views.
